# Cloudflare Pages response headers (served on BOTH Pages projects —
# carres-portal / erp.carresofficial.com and carres-pos / pos.carresofficial.com,
# because the two projects are ONE build of apps/web).
#
# Measured 2026-08-05 before this file existed: production answered with only
# `x-content-type-options` and `referrer-policy`. No HSTS, no clickjacking
# protection, no CSP — on a portal holding customer names, phones, addresses,
# NRIC and Stripe payment paths.
#
# Cloudflare Pages serves `_headers` from the build output root; it is NOT
# bundled by Vite, it is copied from `public/` verbatim. Same mechanism as the
# `_redirects` file sitting beside it.

/*
  # Clickjacking. The portal has no legitimate embedder — nothing frames it.
  X-Frame-Options: DENY

  # The modern equivalent, which X-Frame-Options alone no longer covers.
  Content-Security-Policy: frame-ancestors 'none'

  # Force HTTPS for two years and cover subdomains. Both apexes are already
  # HTTPS-only behind Cloudflare, so this cannot lock anyone out of a working
  # origin. `preload` is deliberately NOT set — that is a one-way submission to
  # a browser-vendor list and is Loo's decision, not a routine header.
  Strict-Transport-Security: max-age=63072000; includeSubDomains

  # Already live; restated here so this file is the ONE place the headers live.
  X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff
  Referrer-Policy: strict-origin-when-cross-origin

  # No page in the portal asks for any of these. Denying them means a
  # compromised dependency cannot silently reach the operator's hardware.
  #
  # `payment=()` is safe TODAY and the reason is structural, not an oversight.
  # This directive governs the browser's in-page Payment Request API (the Apple
  # Pay / Google Pay sheet), and an origin in an allowlist only grants the
  # feature to a frame THIS page embeds. Carres embeds none: Stripe is a QR code
  # carrying the hosted-checkout URL (`StripeCollectModal.tsx`), which the
  # customer scans on their OWN phone — a different device, a different origin,
  # a document this header cannot reach. Measured 2026-08-05: no
  # `@stripe/stripe-js`, no `loadStripe`, no `PaymentElement`, no
  # `PaymentRequest` anywhere in `apps/web`.
  #
  # IF the POS ever moves to Stripe's embedded Payment Element WITH the Apple
  # Pay / Google Pay wallet buttons, change this to:
  #   payment=(self "https://checkout.stripe.com" "https://js.stripe.com")
  # Until then the wallet buttons would render nothing and say nothing about
  # why — this is a header that fails silently and only in production, which is
  # the same reason the CSP below ships Report-Only. Note there is no wildcard
  # and no subdomain matching here: `https://stripe.com` never matches
  # `checkout.stripe.com`, so a half-remembered origin is the same as none.
  Permissions-Policy: camera=(), microphone=(), geolocation=(), payment=(), usb=(), magnetometer=(), gyroscope=(), accelerometer=()

  # CSP is REPORT-ONLY on purpose and this is not timidity — it is the same
  # discipline the repo applies to migrations. A wrong directive here does not
  # warn, it BLANKS the operator's screen, and the app legitimately loads from
  # several third-party origins (Google Fonts CSS + font files, jsDelivr for the
  # @react-pdf font assets, Supabase for storage objects and auth, Stripe for
  # hosted checkout redirects). Enforcing a first-draft policy against a live
  # ERP is exactly the "verify too late" failure §13.2 was written about.
  #
  # `'unsafe-inline'` on style-src is REQUIRED, not laziness: Tailwind + the
  # kit's inline `style` attributes (column widths, the resize drag) emit inline
  # styles, and no nonce reaches them from a static host.
  #
  # TO ENFORCE: deploy, open both apexes, exercise POS checkout / PDF print /
  # photo upload, read the browser console for `[Report Only]` violations, widen
  # the policy to cover any legitimate one, THEN rename this single header to
  # `Content-Security-Policy` and redeploy.
  # `frame-src 'self' blob:` was added 2026-08-06 BEFORE any enforcement, from a
  # static audit rather than a console violation. `GenerateInvoiceOverlay.tsx`
  # builds its preview with `URL.createObjectURL(blob)` (line 197) and feeds it
  # straight to `<iframe src>` (line 401). With no `frame-src`, that iframe falls
  # back to `default-src 'self'`, which does NOT admit `blob:` — so enforcing the
  # policy as it stood would have blanked the invoice preview and said nothing
  # about why. This is exactly the failure mode the Report-Only note above
  # describes, caught one step earlier.
  Content-Security-Policy-Report-Only: default-src 'self'; script-src 'self'; style-src 'self' 'unsafe-inline' https://fonts.googleapis.com; font-src 'self' data: https://fonts.gstatic.com https://cdn.jsdelivr.net; img-src 'self' data: blob: https://kfprgpjpaffedghytstl.supabase.co https://images.unsplash.com; connect-src 'self' https://kfprgpjpaffedghytstl.supabase.co https://api.carresofficial.com https://carres-portal-v2-api.wwch.workers.dev https://cdn.jsdelivr.net; frame-src 'self' blob:; form-action 'self' https://checkout.stripe.com https://pay.stripe.com; frame-ancestors 'none'; base-uri 'self'; object-src 'none'
